WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.150
Repeated eruptions can build a basaltic submarine volcano high enough to

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.250
become an island. If the plate moves relative to the magma

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.670
source, an older volcano leaves that source while a new one

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:14.710
develops above it. This creates an age-progressive island and seamount chain.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.610
Locate the active end before inferring plate motion;

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.510
a line of islands alone is not enough.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:24.090
After volcanic activity ends, cooling and subsidence lower the island.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:27.250
Waves can bevel its top while it remains near sea level.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:32.410
Continued subsidence leaves a flat-topped seamount below the surface, eventually covered

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:36.690
by marine sediment. Eruption builds the volcano, erosion reshapes it, and

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:38.490
cooling lowers its supporting plate.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:40.930
Those processes act at different stages.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.980
The plateau model begins with a rising mass of hot, mostly

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:48.160
solid mantle, not a pipe of liquid magma through the whole

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.960
mantle. Near the base of the lithosphere, melting supplies magma over

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.480
a broad region. Fissures and vents then erupt submarine flood basalts.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.040
Watch successive flows remain in place:

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.360
the plateau is built by accumulating rock, not by raising the

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:02.600
water.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:08.260
Oceanic plateaus cover enormous areas and rise above surrounding seafloor, although

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.980
most remain submerged. The textbook examples include Kerguelen in the southern

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:15.560
Indian Ocean and Ontong Java in the Pacific.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.960
Lecture Fifteen emphasizes that immense basalt volumes accumulated over millions of

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.940
years. A plateau is therefore different from one steep volcanic island,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.600
even when both relate to hot-spot magmatism.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.600
Hawaii and the Tahiti chains illustrate older volcanoes to the northwest

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.140
and younger active volcanoes toward the southeast.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:39.440
That pattern supports northwestward plate movement relative to the source.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.120
Iceland, Tristan da Cunha and the Galápagos provide other examples.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.730
The source also notes debate about which locations are true hot

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:51.420
spots. Use the mapped evidence rather than assigning every island to

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.080
one mechanism.
